SOURCE corpus/SOURCES.md sha256=9715f44dca8dfc2beac94b17e19f0f0206b1efa4d973eb1183650a0f9179794f
# SOURCES — where tameshi learns

Where the lane's external reading lives: what each place teaches, how far to
trust it for which scope, and when to look again. Contract: `SOURCES-CONTRACT-v1.md`
(kaika, 2026-10-19). Admission of claims stays in `QUALITY.md`; this file holds
rows only, and a row is a claim opened in the round that writes it.

## Registry

| id | source | kind | tier | scope | anchor | teaches | status | opened | recheck | used-by |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| S001 | Mutant states and metrics (Stryker Mutator docs) — https://stryker-mutator.io/docs/mutation-testing-elements/mutant-states-and-metrics/ | docs | A | Stryker Mutator's own mutant-state and metric definitions | §Mutant states and §Metrics @ docs page fetched 2026-10-19 | eight mutant states; score = detected / valid x 100 with detected = killed + timeout; compile and runtime errors excluded; a separate score on covered code; test states killing, covering, not covering | mined | 2026-10-19 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/mutation-score-semantics.md, corpus/knowledge/mutation-case-kumitate-sources-check.md |
| S002 | mutation-testing-report-schema.json (stryker-mutator/mutation-testing-elements) — https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stryker-mutator/mutation-testing-elements/master/packages/report-schema/src/mutation-testing-report-schema.json | spec | A | cross-tool mutation report JSON schema | mutation-testing-report-schema.json:64-98 @ master, fetched 2026-10-19 | JSON Schema for cross-tool mutation reports: a status enum of 8 values, killedBy, coveredBy, statusReason and testsCompleted — a ready export shape for a kill-rate ledger | mined | 2026-10-19 | on-release | corpus/knowledge/mutation-score-semantics.md |
| S003 | Incremental (StrykerJS docs) — https://stryker-mutator.io/docs/stryker-js/incremental/ | docs | A | StrykerJS incremental verdict reuse | §Incremental mode and §Limitations @ docs page fetched 2026-10-19 | when a prior verdict may be reused, and the declared blind spots: env vars, dependencies and .snap files are not detected; the dry run stays required | mined | 2026-10-19 | 180d | corpus/knowledge/incremental-pin-disposition.md |
| S004 | cargo-mutants book: Using the results (also in-diff.html and iterate.html) — https://mutants.rs/using-results.html | docs | A | cargo-mutants outcome semantics and run-scoping options | §Tests fail in an clean tree? and §Mutant outcomes @ mutants.rs, fetched 2026-10-19 | outcomes caught, missed, unviable, timeout, with unviable called inconclusive; tests must first pass reliably in a copied tree; --in-diff and --iterate carry explicit cautions | mined | 2026-10-19 | on-release | corpus/knowledge/mutation-score-semantics.md, corpus/knowledge/repeated-control-precondition.md |
| S005 | mutmut changelog HISTORY.rst (boxed/mutmut) — https://raw.githubusercontent.com/boxed/mutmut/main/HISTORY.rst | code | A | mutmut's own recorded harness fixes | HISTORY.rst:13-46 (§3.8.0, §3.7.0) @ main, fetched 2026-10-19 | harness bugs the maintainers recorded, read as method lessons: CPU-time-limited mutants misreported as killed (fixed 3.8.0); env-clearing tests produced false survivors (fixed 3.8.0); per-function hash invalidation (3.7.0) | mined | 2026-10-19 | on-release | corpus/knowledge/mutation-score-semantics.md |
| S006 | shmutant README (BWBama85/shmutant) — https://github.com/BWBama85/shmutant | code | A | shmutant's own bash mutation verdict semantics | README §The three verdicts and §How a row is run @ page currentOid 7eefc73010ad6342b80e3ed43915503e02c90618, fetched 2026-10-19 | a row is old literal, replacement, witness; verdicts killed vs accidental, unapplied, baseline; a non-unique literal is refused; authoritative only for its own semantics (0 stars, 239 commits at open) | mined | 2026-10-19 | 90d | corpus/knowledge/witness-scoped-kill-verdict.md, corpus/knowledge/mutation-score-semantics.md, corpus/lessons/round-12.md |
| S007 | Mutation-Guided LLM-based Test Generation at Meta (Foster, Gulati, Harman et al.; arXiv 2501.12862) — https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12862 | paper | A | Meta ACH's production LLM mutation and test-generation results | Abstract, arXiv:2501.12862v1 (submitted 2025-01-22) | concern-specific LLM mutants that are currently uncaught, then tests that kill them; equivalence agent at precision 0.79 recall 0.47, or 0.95/0.96 with pre-processing; 9,095 mutants, 571 tests, 73% accepted | queued | 2026-10-19 | 365d | — |
| S008 | Practical Mutation Testing at Scale (Petrović, Ivanković, Fraser, Just; arXiv 2102.11378) — https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.11378 | paper | A | Google's diff-scoped mutation testing at code review | Abstract, arXiv:2102.11378v2 (v1 2021-02-22, last revised 2021-02-26) | diff-scoped mutation at code review; mutants filtered and capped per line and per review; operators chosen by historical productivity; 24,000+ developers on 1,000+ projects | mined | 2026-10-19 | 365d | corpus/knowledge/mutation-case-kumitate-sources-check.md |
| S009 | Trivial Compiler Equivalence (Papadakis, Jia, Harman, Le Traon; ICSE 2015) — https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/1499169/ | paper | A | TCE's equivalent and duplicate mutant detection rates | Abstract, UCL Discovery eprint 1499169 (ICSE 2015; page fetched 2026-10-19) | identical compiled output marks a mutant as equivalent or duplicate; on large programs it discards over 7% equivalent and 21% duplicated mutants, and finds about 30% of all equivalents | queued | 2026-10-19 | 730d | — |
| S010 | Threats to the validity of mutation-based test assessment (Papadakis, Henard, Harman, Jia, Le Traon; ISSTA 2016) — https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/1508136/ | paper | A | subsumption threat rates in mutation-based test assessment | Abstract, UCL Discovery eprint 1508136 (ISSTA 2016; page fetched 2026-10-19) | mixing subsuming and subsumed mutants distorts scores: Type I errors about 62% of the time without countermeasures; 68% of surveyed studies were exposed | queued | 2026-10-19 | 730d | — |
| S011 | Mitigating the Effects of Flaky Tests on Mutation Testing (Shi, Bell, Marinov; ISSTA 2019) — https://mir.cs.illinois.edu/awshi2/publications/ISSTA2019.pdf | paper | A | flakiness impact on mutation verdicts | Abstract, ISSTA 2019 PDF p.1 (fetched 2026-10-19; DOI 10.1145/3293882.3330568) | kill verdicts assume determinism: scores vary about four points between runs, 9% of mutant-test pairs are unknown, 22% of statements are covered nondeterministically, and reruns cut unknown mutants by 79.4% | mined | 2026-10-19 | 730d | corpus/lessons/round-12.md |
| S012 | Oracles That Cannot Fail (Canedo; arXiv 2608.17214) — https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17214 | paper | B | oracle anchoring as a survivor class (single-system preprint, one author) | Abstract, arXiv:2608.17214v1 (submitted 2026-08-17) | names state-anchored oracles, whose expectation flows from the code under mutation so the comparison cancels out; re-anchoring one oracle recovered 8 of 46 mutants; measured on one system (366 mutants) | queued | 2026-10-19 | 90d | — |
| S013 | Jest CLI Options (Jest 30.5) — https://jestjs.io/docs/cli | docs | A | Jest 30.5 CLI semantics | §Options, --collectTests and --ci @ Version 30.5 (fetched 2026-10-19) | --ci fails on a new snapshot instead of writing it; --randomize with --seed reproduces order-dependent failures; --collectTests counts tests without executing them and its counts match a run where every selected test passes | queued | 2026-10-19 | on-release | — |
| S014 | insta: Advanced Features (also the redactions page) — https://insta.rs/docs/advanced/ | docs | A | insta snapshot-update and orphan-check semantics | §Controlling Snapshot Updating and §Handling Unused Snapshots @ docs page fetched 2026-10-19 | INSTA_UPDATE=auto resolves to no in CI and new otherwise; --unreferenced=auto rejects orphan snapshots in CI; redactions replace volatile values by selector | queued | 2026-10-19 | on-release | — |
| S015 | Cram: It's test time — https://bitheap.org/cram/ | docs | A | cram's transcript matching and environment resets | §Usage: output matching suffixes and environment resets (fetched 2026-10-19) | shell-transcript golden tests: literal match first, with opt-in (re), (glob), (no-eol), (esc) suffixes; the runner resets TMPDIR, LANG/LC_ALL/LANGUAGE=C, TZ=GMT, COLUMNS=80; exit 80 = skipped | queued | 2026-10-19 | 365d | — |
| S016 | testscript package doc (rogpeppe/go-internal) — https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rogpeppe/go-internal/master/testscript/doc.go | code | A | testscript's fixture and environment contract | testscript/doc.go:56-61 @ master, fetched 2026-10-19 | txtar scripts carry commands plus fixture files; each runs in a fresh $WORK with HOME=/no-home and TMPDIR=$WORK/.tmp; a ! prefix asserts failure; [net] and [exec:prog] conditions gate cases | queued | 2026-10-19 | 365d | — |
| S017 | bats-core: Writing tests — https://bats-core.readthedocs.io/en/stable/writing-tests.html | docs | A | bats-core test isolation and the focus guard | §Special tags, Focusing on tests with bats:focus tag @ stable docs (fetched 2026-10-19) | each test runs in its own process (the file is evaluated n+1 times); focus mode overrides a successful run's exit code to 1 so a committed bats:focus tag cannot silently run a subset in CI | queued | 2026-10-19 | on-release | — |
| S018 | Hypothesis API Reference — https://hypothesis.readthedocs.io/en/latest/reference/api.html | docs | A | Hypothesis settings profiles and CI defaults | §Settings, Built-in profiles (the ci profile) @ latest docs (fetched 2026-10-19) | a built-in ci profile activates whenever CI is set: derandomize=True, deadline=None, database=None, print_blob=True; @example(...).xfail() checks that a test can fail; shared read-only example databases | queued | 2026-10-19 | on-release | — |
| S019 | Property-Based Testing in Practice (Goldstein, Cutler, Dickstein, Pierce, Head; ICSE 2024) — https://harrisongoldste.in/papers/icse24-pbt-in-practice.pdf | paper | A | PBT practice at one company (Jane Street interviews) | Abstract, ICSE 2024 PDF p.1 (fetched 2026-10-19) | 30 interviews: PBT pays off on complex code; its weaknesses are generator effort (6/30 tedious, 7/30 high-effort) and no visible feedback on test effectiveness; shrinking interfaces and effectiveness evaluation are open problems | queued | 2026-10-19 | 730d | — |
